@page "/agent-chat"

<CatHead Title="Asking an agent">
    The <a href="/chat">chat</a>, extended for an agent's turn: what it thought, which tools it ran, an
    answer that streams in, where the answer came from, and what the reader can do with it next. The
    composer is sized for asking rather than chatting. Every word is the app's, and only the suggestion
    after the caret and the toolbar over a selection need the script.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.Turn" Title="An agent's turn" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.chat-message--agent</code> sets the answer as prose on the thread's ground rather than in a
        bubble: an agent's answer is read like a document, and a bubble around four paragraphs and a code
        block reads as a wall. The reader's own messages stay <code>--mine</code> bubbles. Inside the
        body, in order: <a href="/agent-steps"><code>details.reasoning</code></a>, the
        <a href="/agent-steps"><code>.agent-steps</code></a> it took, the answer, its sources, and what to
        do next.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.citation</code> is a numbered button inline in the answer, opening a
        <a href="/popover"><code>.popover</code></a> on the source through <code>popovertarget</code>, so it
        needs no script. Give each an <code>aria-label</code>; the digit alone reads as a number.
        <code>.citation-sources</code> lists the same sources under the answer as <code>.source-card</code>
        links, with <code>.source-card-n</code> matching the citation.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.chat-feedback</code> is the row under the answer: copy, regenerate, a rating with
        <code>aria-pressed</code>, and <code>.chat-versions</code> to page between regenerated answers. Its
        first glyph lines up with the text above it. <a href="/chat"><code>.chat-quick-replies</code></a>
        offers the likely next questions.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.ContextUsage" Title="What the context holds" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; rest the pointer on the ring, or tab to it.</strong> The
        <a href="/agent-status"><code>.context-ring</code></a> in a ghost button opens a hover card
        (<a href="/popover"><code>data-popover</code> with <code>data-popover-hover</code></a>); a click
        pins it. <code>.context-usage</code> on the <code>.popover</code> is what the window is made of:
        <code>.context-usage-head</code> with the total, a <a href="/bars"><code>.meter</code></a> in
        the ring's colour, <code>dl.context-usage-rows</code> &mdash; input, output, reasoning and cached
        tokens, with what each cost, the figures in shared columns &mdash; and
        <code>.context-usage-foot</code> for the total cost.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.Checkpoint" Title="A checkpoint" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        A <a href="/structure"><code>.divider-labelled</code></a> across the thread marks a point the
        conversation and its changes can go back to: a bookmark icon, the time, and a button that
        restores to there. A button in the break keeps its own case.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.Streaming" Title="While the answer streams" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.chat-bubble--streaming</code> puts a caret after the last block while text is still arriving.
        The app appends the text and removes the class when the answer is complete; mark the bubble
        <code>aria-busy</code> meanwhile, so a screen reader in the log waits for the whole answer rather
        than reading every word as it lands. An <a href="/agent-status">agent status</a> under it says what
        the agent is doing.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.Prompt" Title="The prompt" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.chat-composer--prompt</code> draws a <a href="/chat"><code>.chat-composer</code></a> as one
        rounded field: the text on top, <code>.chat-composer-tools</code> under it for attachments, toggles
        as <code>button.chip</code> with <code>aria-pressed</code>, and a model choice, and
        <code>.chat-composer-send</code> holding the send button at the end. The field's focus ring is the
        composer's, so the whole box lights up as one control.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.PromptToolbar" Title="The prompt's toolbar" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Under the text, in order: a <a href="/menu"><code>.menu</code></a> behind &ldquo;+&rdquo; for what can
        be added &mdash; a file, a screenshot, a link &mdash; the model, and in
        <code>.chat-composer-send</code> the screenshot, the <a href="/voice"><code>.btn-mic</code></a> and
        the submit button. What may be attached is said once, as a <code>.form-hint</code> the field names in
        <code>aria-describedby</code>; a file that breaks the limit is a <code>.form-error</code> in the same
        place, saying which file and why.
    </p>
    <p>
        The submit button is send, stop or retry depending on where the answer is. Write all three inside
        it, each in a span whose <code>data-when</code> names the states it belongs to, and set
        <code>data-state</code> on <code>.chat-composer-send</code> to <code>idle</code>,
        <code>submitted</code>, <code>streaming</code> or <code>error</code>: the stylesheet shows the one
        that matches. A hidden span is out of the accessibility tree too, so the button's name is the
        shown state's words; give each icon <code>aria-hidden</code>, or its glyph joins the name.
        Without <code>data-state</code> it is idle.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.SendStates" Title="Send, stop and retry" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        The same button in each of the four states. <code>submitted</code> &mdash; sent, nothing back yet
        &mdash; and <code>streaming</code> both offer stop here, which is why one span names both.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.ModelPicker" Title="Choosing the model" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; press the model.</strong> The button in the toolbar names the model in use and
        opens a <a href="/popover"><code>.popover--flush</code></a> holding a
        <a href="/form-combo"><code>.form-combo--inline</code></a>: a search over the models, grouped by
        where they run under <code>.form-combo-group</code>. Each <code>.form-combo-option</code> leads with
        its provider's icon, puts a <code>.form-combo-option-note</code> under the name inside
        <code>.form-combo-option-text</code>, and ends with its shortcut in a
        <a href="/palette"><code>.kbd</code></a>. The shortcut is the app's to bind. Close the popover when
        a model is picked and write its name into the button.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.GhostText" Title="Suggesting the rest" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; click into the field, then type or press Tab.</strong> Put the textarea and an
        empty <code>.ghost-text</code> with <code>aria-hidden</code> in a <code>.ghost-field</code>, and the
        suggestion in the textarea's <code>data-suggestion</code>. While the field has the focus, the caret
        is at the end and what is typed is the start of the suggestion, ignoring case, the rest shows after
        the caret in the placeholder's colour. Tab writes it into the value and fires <code>input</code>,
        so a binding on <code>oninput</code> sees it; Escape dismisses it, and so does typing something it
        does not start with, until the app writes a different suggestion.
    </p>
    <p>
        Update <code>data-suggestion</code> as the reader types, and remove it when there is nothing to
        suggest. Say that Tab accepts in a hint the field names in <code>aria-describedby</code>: the ghost
        is not read out. Textarea only; the browser's undo does not take an accepted suggestion back.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.SelectionToolbar" Title="Acting on a selection" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; select a few words of the answer.</strong> <code>data-selection-toolbar</code>
        on the thread names a <code>.selection-toolbar</code> the app wrote, a
        <code>popover="manual"</code> with <code>role="toolbar"</code>. Once text inside the thread is
        selected, the script opens it above the selection &mdash; below it near the top of the screen,
        never past an edge &mdash; and closes it when the selection goes, on Escape and on a scroll. A
        selection is no element and cannot be an anchor, so this one panel is placed by the script.
    </p>
    <p>
        A press on a control with <code>data-selection-action</code> closes it and fires
        <code>sedna-selection</code> on the toolbar &mdash; <code>&#64;onsedna-selection</code> in Razor
        &mdash; with the <code>action</code>, the selected <code>text</code>, the
        <code>data-selection-item</code> of the message it came from as <code>item</code>, and the
        thread's id as <code>source</code>. From the keyboard, Alt+F10 moves into the toolbar and the arrow
        keys move along it; say so where the reader will find it. <code>.selection-toolbar-sep</code>
        divides its groups.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentChat.PromptBusy" Title="While the agent writes" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Put <a href="/agent-status"><code>.beam</code></a> on the composer while an answer is being written,
        and swap send for stop. The reader can keep typing the next question.
    </p>
</CatExample>
